{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And to Sarah he said.</b> Avimelekh — for her honor, to appease her: 'Behold, I have done you this honor — I gave money to your brother, about whom you said, \"He is my brother.\" Behold, the money and this honor <b>are for you a covering of the eyes</b>.'" "<b>For all who are with you.</b> Let their eyes be covered so they do not disparage you. For had I sent you back empty-handed, they would have had grounds to say, \"After he had his way with her he sent her back.\" But now that I had to squander money and appease you, they will know that I returned you against my will and by way of a miracle." "<b>And all.</b> And with all who come into the world." "<b>And you will be reproved.</b> May you have an opening of the mouth to argue and to make these matters clear. And the term הוֹכָחָה everywhere means clarification of matters, and in לַעַ\"ז אשׁפרוֹ\"בִּיר. And Onkelos translated it in a different direction. But the language of Scripture fits the Targum thus: 'Behold, for you is a covering of honor upon my eyes, which ruled over you and over all who are with you' — and therefore he translated וַחֲזֵית יָתִיךְ וְיַת כָּל דְּעִמָּךְ. And there are those who say otherwise, but the straightforward sense of Scripture I have explained."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {3 "The la'az אשׁפרוֹ\"בִּיר is Old French esprover, 'to prove/demonstrate.' The comment engages Onkelos's reading of ונכחת as 'and you will see' rather than 'and you will be reproved'; Rashi shows both readings and sides with the plain sense."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:50.931346622Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}